{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_016iwDvNWAEaUWgQTfkitGox","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Translation conventions for sub-agents\n2\t\n3\tYou are translating ONE C source file from the zstd library (in `c_src/src/...`)\n4\tinto ONE Rust file in this crate. The crate is a `cdylib` reproducing the C\n5\tlibrary's public ABI **byte-for-byte**.\n6\t\n7\t## Hard rules\n8\t- Target platform: little-endian 64-bit Linux. You may assume `size_t`/`usize` = 8 bytes.\n9\t- Every C function that is a GLOBAL symbol (appears in the provided symbol list)\n10\t  MUST be exported as:\n11\t  ```rust\n12\t  #[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n13\t  pub unsafe extern \"C\" fn NAME(args...) -> ret { ... }\n14\t  ```\n15\t  The Rust name MUST exactly equal the linker symbol name in the list.\n16\t- Preserve EXACT C function signatures using core::ffi types\n17\t  (`c_void`, `c_int`=i32, `c_uint`=u32, `size_t`=usize, `c_char`=i8, etc.).\n18\t  Use `*const`/`*mut` for pointers.\n19\t- Do NOT fix bugs. Reproduce behavior, error-check order, and integer overflow\n20\t  (use `wrapping_*` where C relies on wraparound) exactly.\n21\t- `static`/file-local C functions -> private (non-`no_mangle`) Rust fns.\n22\t- Reproduce error codes as `(-(code as isize)) as usize`. Error enum values are\n23\t  in `crate::common::error::code`. `ERR_isError(x)` == `x > error(MAXCODE)`.\n24\t- Memory: use these from `crate::common::allocations`:\n25\t  `malloc, calloc, free, memcpy, memmove, memset, qsort` (libc), and\n26\t  `ZSTD_customMem`, `zstd_custom_malloc/calloc/free`.\n27\t- Unaligned LE reads/writes: use helpers in `crate::common::mem`\n28\t  (`mem_read_le16/32/64`, `mem_read16/32/64`, `mem_write*`, etc.). Or just use\n29\t  `core::ptr::copy_nonoverlapping`. On LE64 native reads are fine.\n30\t- For structs shared across the ABI boundary, use `#[repr(C)]`.\n31\t- Put `#![allow(non_snake_case, non_camel_case_types, non_upper_case_globals, dead_code, unused_mut, unused_assignments, unused_parens)]` concerns at crate root (already set) — you can still add `#[allow(...)]` locally if needed.\n32\t\n33\t## Legacy files (zstd_vNN.c)\n34\tThese are self-contained: they define their OWN internal FSE/HUF/ZBUFF code.\n35\tTranslate the WHOLE file into `src/legacy/zstd_vNN.rs`. Internal helper\n36\tfunctions/types stay private to the module. Only the symbols in the provided\n37\tlist get `#[unsafe(no_mangle)]`. Note some exported names are prefixed\n38\t`FSEv05_`, `HUFv05_`, `ZBUFFv06_`, etc. — export those exactly as listed.\n39\t\n40\t## Verification\n41\tYour file must compile as part of `cargo build --release`. After writing it,\n42\tadd `pub mod zstd_vNN;` is handled by the coordinator — you only write the .rs.\n43\tDo NOT edit files outside your assigned .rs unless told.\n44\tDo NOT modify anything under c_src/.\n45\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Qm6T4zjMKQnFK4VuZVvbvp","session_id":"d726b7a2-9f1b-40fa-9ece-fa56fda2dd37","uuid":"6d9fe174-acf8-47d5-a218-857cfb771cb3","timestamp":"2026-08-05T03:58:10.482Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Translate legacy zstd_v01"}
